However, without such a program, the task can't be solved directly by means of programming.--------------------Here is the code to generate assert statements given the C# source code:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class AssertionStatementGenerator {
    private readonly Regex _commentRegex = new Regex(@"^\s*//\s*@@@\s*(.*)");
    
    public string GenerateAssertionStatementForSource(string sourceCode) {
        var assertStatements = new List<string>();
        
        var lines = sourceCode.Split('\n');
        bool inFunction = false;
        string functionName = "";
        string variablesWithSizes = "";
        string lastOperation = "";
        string returnValue = "";

        foreach (var line in lines) {
            var trimmedLine = line.Trim();
            if (inFunction && trimmedLine.StartsWith("{")) {
                inFunction = false;
                var matches = _commentRegex.Matches(trimmedLine);
                foreach (Match match in matches) {
                    var comment = match.Groups[1].Value;
                    assertStatements.Add($"Debug.Assert({variablesWithSizes} Environment.NewLine \t\t \"{returnValue}\" {lastOperation} \"{comment}\");");
                }
            } else if (trimmedLine.StartsWith("public static")) {
                inFunction = true;
                returnValue = "";
                functionName = trimmedLine.Split(' ')[2].Split('(')[0];
                variablesWithSizes = "";
                lastOperation = "";
            } else if (trimmedLine.StartsWith("//")) {
                var matches = _commentRegex.Matches(trimmedLine);
                foreach (Match match in matches) {
                    var comment = match.Groups[1].Value;
                    assertStatements.Add($"Debug.Assert({variablesWithSizes} Environment.NewLine \t\t \"{returnValue}\" {lastOperation} \"{comment}\");");
                }
            } else if (inFunction && trimmedLine.Contains("return")) {
                returnValue = trimmedLine.Split(' ')[1];
            } else if (inFunction && trimmedLine.Contains("=")) {
                lastOperation = "==";
                var variablesOnBothSide = trimmedLine.Split('=');
                variablesWithSizes += $"OLD({variablesOnBothSide[0].Trim()}) {variablesOnBothSide[0]}= {variablesOnBothSide[1]} == Environment.NewLine \t\t";
            }
        }

        return string.Join("\n", assertStatements);
    }
}
```

Usage:

```csharp
string sourceCode = File.ReadAllText("Program.cs");
AssertionStatementGenerator assGen = new AssertionStatementGenerator();
string assertStatements = assGen.GenerateAssertionStatementForSource(sourceCode);
Console.WriteLine(assertStatements);
```

This program will generate assert statements based on the comments that start with "@@@". The comments are expected to be a part of the code that is related to the assertion statement. If the condition for the assertion statement accesses the value of variables at the beginning of the function, it uses the `OLD(variable_name)` syntax. The return value of the function can be accessed using `RET` variable.

The source code is tokenized and read in line-by-line, keeping track of the inFunction variable to know when we are inside a function and which function it is, the variablesWithSizes string keeping track of the values of variables and also the last operation performed and the current return value. If a comment with "@@@" is encountered inside a function or right after a function block ({}), it is captured and an assertion statement is made based on the variables and operation performed at that point, and the return value of the function.--------------------<code>
